Rendering:

    Idea 1> Grid represents the state of the whole screen.
         2> Each window builds a per frame subgrid which represents the screen area 
         that has changed within that window in the current frame.
         3> To render, we calculate the difference between the window grids and the screen 
         grids. We send the diff to the ui frontend (currently we only have the terminal)
         and update the screen grid.

    Spetial cases: Potential Optimizations:
        1> scrolling: We must only create the per window subgrid for the part of the screen 
        that has been scrolled in.
        The other parts diff can be calculated within the grid itself. 
        2> We should maybe sort the diffs by row (use stable sort, the average case will be sorted),
        assuming the terminal updates the screen ((left, top) to (right, bottom));

The difficulty is calculating the subgrid range.

Every time the user changes a buffer the range of change is given by (top_changed, bot_changed);

window->top_line is the buffer line which corresponds to the windows top line.
we must calculate the intersection of (top_line, one_after_bot_line) and (top_change, bot_changed);

    1> if it is empty there is nothing to do.
    2> otherwise, we must map the the intersection which is in buffer space to grid space;


Should I have a diff_grid per window?;
    1> Disadvantages: More memory (but slighly);
    2> Advantages: Multithreaded diff calculation;
